If a file named exists in the directory of the  current  request,
will restrict access to documents in that directory using the RFC
2617 HTTP authentication scheme.  Note: This does not recursively
protect  any  sub‐directories.  The file contains lines delimited
with a colon containing user names and passwords hashed with  for
example:      heather:$1$pZWI4tH/$DzDPl63i6VvVRv2lJNV7k1      je‐
remy:A.xewbx2DpQ8I On the utility may be used to generate  hashed
passwords.  While distributed with has support for HTTP Basic Au‐
thorization  enabled  by default, in the portable distribution it
is excluded.  Compile with on the compiler command line to enable
this support.  It may require linking with the crypt library, us‐
ing On supports by default.  The support can be disabled with the
compilation option.  Upon occurrence,  reports  two  HTTP  status
codes  to  as  failures: (‘‘Unauthorized’’) and (‘‘Forbidden’’) .
Of these, is the one received upon authorization failure with the
HTTP Basic Authorization mechanism.  A  successful  authorization
decreases the counter kept by Note that the implementation of the
HTTP  Basic  Authorization mechanism uses a redirection; a status
code is always initially received.  Therefore,  a  single  autho‐
rization  failure  of is reported as two failures to but no fail‐
ures are recorded upon successful authorization due  to  the  de‐
crease  of  the  failure  counter.   has  support for TLSv1.1 and
